※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none host=host.c
※ pl4g-test: args -shared

※ `@[relro]` in a shared object: the constructors write it, and what the object
※ offers reads it -- under the same rule the checker holds an executable to.
※
※ **The loader runs a shared object's constructors, after it has acted on
※ `PT_GNU_RELRO`**, so the pages that header names are read-only before any of
※ them runs, and what they write cannot be there.  The variables stay in a
※ section of their own, `.pl4g.relro`, which is writable data of the object
※ and not in that header: nothing makes them read-only once the constructors
※ have run.  Doing it would take a function of the object's own run last of
※ its constructors and a segment of its own for the pages, in a process whose
※ other code -- the host's, written in anything -- may write anything anyway;
※ what keeps the object's own code from writing them is the checker, which is
※ the same as in an executable.

let base: i64 = 2i64

@[relro]
let scale: mut i64 = 1i64

@[relro]
let chosen: mut &static i64 = &base

@[constructor, impure]
fn begin():
    scale ← 20i64

@[visible, cdecl]
fn run(n: i64) → i64:
    n × scale + chosen⌖
